    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\n\n`LspAttach` and `LspDetach` are too low-level to be used comfortably in user configs.\n\nMost of the user-facing LSP API is server-agnostic. Take `vim.lsp.buf.definition()`: you don't tell it which server to ask. Neovim queries all attached servers that support `textDocument/definition` and decides what to do with the results. It's a high-level primitive.\n\nHowever, [the recommended place](https://neovim.io/doc/user/lsp/#lsp-attach) to set up keymaps for these functions is the `LspAttach` autocommand, which is client-specific: it fires separately for **every** server that attaches to a buffer. A typical config that maps `gd` to `vim.lsp.buf.definition()` looks like this:\n\n```lua\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('LspAttach', {\n  callback = function(args)\n    local bufnr = args.buf\n    local client = vim.lsp.get_client_by_id(args.data.client_id)\n    if client:supports_method('textDocument/definition', bufnr) then\n      vim.keymap.set('n', 'gd', vim.lsp.buf.definition, { buf = bufnr })\n    end\n  end,\n})\n```\n\nThis approach has several problems:\n\n1. **Redundant work.** `vim.keymap.set` runs every time a server that supports the method attaches. We only need to map `gd` once, because `vim.lsp.buf.definition()` doesn't care which server provides the result.\n\n2. **It doesn't handle dynamic registration (`client/registerCapability`).** A server may register `textDocument/definition` after it has attached. In that case `supports_method()` returns `false` inside `LspAttach`, and the keymap is never created.\n\n3. **Cleanup in `LspDetach` is unreasonably hard.** A user who wants to remove `gd` when the server goes away will naturally write:\n\n```lua\n   vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('LspDetach', {\n     callback = function(args)\n       local bufnr = args.buf\n       local client = vim.lsp.get_client_by_id(args.data.client_id)\n       if client:supports_method('textDocument/definition', bufnr) then\n         vim.keymap.del('n', 'gd', { buf = bufnr })\n       end\n     end,\n   })\n```\n\n   This is wrong. Suppose two servers, `foo` and `bar`, are attached to the buffer and both support `textDocument/definition`. Running `:lsp disable foo` removes `gd`, even though `bar` is still attached and can still handle it.\n\n   Doing this correctly requires tracking which attached clients support which methods per buffer (including dynamically registered ones), which is effectively a small framework. It can't be done in a few lines.\n\nAs a result, every user who wants a correct LSP setup has to carry the same boilerplate in their config. That boilerplate is hard to read and requires detailed knowledge of how Neovim and LSP servers interact under the hood.\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nSince `vim.lsp.buf` already provides server-agnostic functions that handle multiple servers internally, there should also be a server-agnostic event to go with them.\n\nI propose a single `LspMethod` event that tracks whether a method is available in a buffer, regardless of which client provides it:\n\n- it fires with `available = true` when the **first** client supporting the method becomes available in the buffer (via attach or `client/registerCapability`);\n- it fires with `available = false` when the **last** client supporting the method stops providing it (via detach or `client/unregisterCapability`).\n\nThe autocmd pattern is the method name, and `args.data` contains `{ available: boolean }`:\n\n```lua\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('LspMethod', {\n  pattern = 'textDocument/definition',\n  callback = function(args)\n    if args.data.available then\n      vim.keymap.set('n', 'gd', vim.lsp.buf.definition, { buf = args.buf })\n    else\n      vim.keymap.del('n', 'gd', { buf = args.buf })\n    end\n  end,\n})\n```\n\nThis fixes all three problems:\n\n- the callback runs once per buffer and method, not once per client;\n- dynamic registration is handled;\n- cleanup is correct by construction.\n\nIn practice, configs rarely need the client object for anything except checking which methods it supports. With these events, buffer-local LSP setup could be expressed in terms of methods rather than clients, which matches how `vim.lsp.buf.*` already works.",
